<details class="hopfield-demo face-memory-demo" open>
  <summary>
    <h2 id="hopfield-title">Opfield Machine Learning Demo <span>· Opfield Networks</span></h2>
    <span class="demo-chevron" aria-hidden="true">⌄</span>
  </summary>
  <div class="face-memory-workspace">
    <figure class="face-memory-figure">
      <canvas
        id="face-memory"
        width="300"
        height="300"
        role="img"
        aria-label="Alessandro’s pixelated face with the left half missing"
        data-portrait="{{ '/assets/img/tugne-demo-cutout.png' | relative_url }}"
      ></canvas>
      <figcaption id="face-caption">Half a face. Can the network remember the rest?</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="face-memory-explanation">
      <div class="face-recall-controls">
        <button type="button" id="retrieve-pattern" class="demo-button" disabled>Retrieve Pattern</button>
        <p id="recall-status" role="status" aria-live="polite">Loading the portrait…</p>
        <span class="face-step-count">(Steps: <span id="step-counter">0</span>/3000)</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        A Hopfield network is an associative memory inspired by the Ising model. Its neurons interact to reach stable, low-energy states, allowing a
        partial pattern to recover a stored memory.
      </p>
      <p>
        This network has learned a 50 × 50 binary portrait of me. The left half starts blank; click <strong>Retrieve Pattern</strong> to watch
        asynchronous neuron updates reconstruct it.
      </p>
      <p class="face-energy" aria-label="Energy equals minus one half the sum over distinct neurons of weight i j times state i times state j">
        E = −½ ∑<sub>i ≠ j</sub> W<sub>ij</sub> s<sub>i</sub> s<sub>j</sub>
      </p>
      <p class="face-demo-note">
        A small educational demonstration inspired by <a href="https://physics-morris.github.io/">Morris Huang’s website</a>. The network recalls a
        learned image; it does not generate a new face.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <noscript><p>Enable JavaScript to run the face-memory demonstration in your browser.</p></noscript>
</details>
